WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
How do you get to reading more
than a million words a year in

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.040
the language you're learning?

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.210
Is that even possible?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.170
Reading is key to so many things in life.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.310
In our own language,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:15.720
people who read well have
more socioeconomic success.

00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:17.160
The statistics prove this.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.750
We have the case of Elon Musk, who
apparently read two books a day.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.860
I think the average book has
70-, 80-, 90,000 words in it.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.070
250, 300 pages or so.

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:32.490
Apparently Warren Buffet
reads 500 pages a day.

00:00:32.925 --> 00:00:38.355
So those two gentlemen read
30 plus million words a year.

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:42.675
The average person, according to
statistics, reads—in the US or in

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:47.895
typical European country—14, 15 books
a year, a little more than one a month.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:49.965
This works out to a little
over a million words a year.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.480
Just in terms of books. But, of course,
we read all kinds of other stuff.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.280
We read newspaper articles online.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.320
We read Twitter, Facebook, and so forth.

00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:03.000
So we easily read more than a million
words a year in our own language.

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:05.489
But how does this relate
to language learning?

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:10.170
I think reading is tremendously
powerful in language learning.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.729
I always feel that when I get to
where I can read a book away from

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:18.899
the computer without the help of LingQ,
or, you know, online dictionaries.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.070
That's a major milestone
in my learning activity.

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:27.179
So what about the strategy of
reading a million words in a

00:01:27.184 --> 00:01:28.289
language that we're learning?

00:01:28.965 --> 00:01:33.495
Obviously it's much more difficult to
read in the language we're learning.

00:01:33.585 --> 00:01:38.175
The more different the language is,
and especially if the writing system is

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.455
different, it's a lot more difficult.

00:01:40.695 --> 00:01:45.405
So if we say that the average person
reads, say, a book a month, and some

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:49.785
people read a book a week, if I
get to where I, you know, this milestone

00:01:49.785 --> 00:01:53.325
of reading a book in the language
that I'm learning, I'm not reading a

00:01:53.325 --> 00:01:55.335
book a week or even a book a month.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.130
In that language, at least
not for a long, long time.

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:03.899
So I would say, first of all, we
have to make a distinction between

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:07.410
someone like me who is a bit of a
dilettante, and I'm working on Persian and

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.269
Arabic, and I throw in some Polish,
versus someone who's learning English.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.905
I think for those of you out there who
are learning English, who are working

00:02:16.905 --> 00:02:20.894
on improving your English, you want
to be as good as possible in English.

00:02:20.894 --> 00:02:24.584
Maybe you need it for school
or you need it for work.

00:02:25.005 --> 00:02:31.275
Then I think the objective is trying to
read at least one book in English a month.

00:02:31.984 --> 00:02:37.085
It's realistic and it would be very
beneficial to your language skills.

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:40.984
And the same is true for someone
who is dedicated to one language,

00:02:41.015 --> 00:02:42.875
French, Spanish, German, Chinese.

00:02:43.204 --> 00:02:46.595
If you can get yourself to where
you can read a book a month in

00:02:46.595 --> 00:02:51.605
your target language, it'll have
tremendous benefits. Because reading is

00:02:51.605 --> 00:02:53.195
connected to listening and speaking.

00:02:53.615 --> 00:02:55.875
We have to remember that
for most of history

00:02:56.245 --> 00:03:00.715
humans didn't—and whatever animals
preceded us on the evolutionary

00:03:00.775 --> 00:03:02.665
scale—obviously didn't read.

00:03:02.665 --> 00:03:05.395
It's not something
natural to human beings.

00:03:05.875 --> 00:03:08.905
And for most of that period of
history, when we had writing,

00:03:08.935 --> 00:03:09.835
most people didn't read.

00:03:09.925 --> 00:03:13.644
So the fact that we are able to
read is a tremendous achievement. And

00:03:13.704 --> 00:03:16.855
all kinds of research shows that 

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.239
it's connected with sound. That when
we develop our ability to read,

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:24.810
we first have to develop this—
what they call "phonemic awareness"

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.989
The connection between the
written word and the sound.

00:03:28.049 --> 00:03:30.089
And this takes time to develop.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.339
Struggling readers don't hear the
sounds very well, so in dealing

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.700
with dyslexic people, or people who
struggle to read in their own language,

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.649
increasingly educators are using
teaching methods that connect the sound

00:03:43.965 --> 00:03:46.695
to the written word. It's extremely important.

00:03:46.875 --> 00:03:52.005
So as a language learner, we are in
the situation of a struggling reader.

00:03:52.245 --> 00:03:56.565
We're not struggling to read in our own
language with all of the consequences

00:03:56.565 --> 00:03:59.234
that has for socioeconomic success.

00:03:59.625 --> 00:04:02.355
And it's a major problem in societies.

00:04:02.355 --> 00:04:06.795
You know, weak readers have
difficulty. They're overrepresented

00:04:06.795 --> 00:04:08.535
in prisons, and so on and so forth.

00:04:09.084 --> 00:04:12.714
Not always, like I know people who don't
read well, who are very successful

00:04:12.714 --> 00:04:16.825
at what they do. But largely speaking,
reading is considered a shortcut

00:04:16.825 --> 00:04:19.315
to greater socioeconomic success.

00:04:19.644 --> 00:04:24.055
If we can improve the performance
of poor readers, it'll have a major

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.164
impact for those individuals
and also for society as a whole.

00:04:28.495 --> 00:04:33.235
But getting back to language learning.
We want to use the same approach in order

00:04:33.235 --> 00:04:36.115
to improve our language learning skills.

00:04:36.145 --> 00:04:37.885
So connect audio with

00:04:38.195 --> 00:04:39.635
text as much as possible.

00:04:39.815 --> 00:04:43.835
That's why at LingQ, for every item
that we have in our libraries,

00:04:44.135 --> 00:04:45.784
there is audio and text.

00:04:45.965 --> 00:04:50.104
That's why we now, we enable
people to grab an MP3 file

00:04:50.104 --> 00:04:54.125
somewhere or from a YouTube video,
or whatever, for their own use.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.255
Of course, there's a limit
to what they can share.

00:04:56.625 --> 00:04:57.405
Bring it in.

00:04:57.405 --> 00:05:00.495
There's an automatic
transcription created, which

00:05:00.495 --> 00:05:02.565
then you can study as a lesson.

00:05:02.595 --> 00:05:06.405
The idea is that if you're focused
on one language, you want to get to a

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:08.505
level where you can read a book a month.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.560
That will really propel your language
skills forward because whenever

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.690
you read, you are subvocalizing.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:21.120
We know, science tells us, you know,
MRIs of the brain tell us that the

00:05:21.125 --> 00:05:23.580
same portion of our brain is activated

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.250
when we read as when we speak or
listen. And this is true whether the

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:33.980
writing system is a pictographic system
like Chinese characters or a syllabary

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:39.410
like the Japanese writing system or
a pure alphabet like Hangul in Korean

00:05:39.415 --> 00:05:43.430
or the Latin alphabet or the Arabic
script. The brain performs the same way.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:46.670
Text connects to audio.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:52.610
Certainly if we're fairly good in the
language we can follow along at LingQ.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:56.930
I very much encourage people to
import content of interest to

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.030
LingQ where they also have the audio.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:03.560
Remember that one key
to reading success is that

00:06:03.565 --> 00:06:05.270
the content be of interest.

00:06:05.625 --> 00:06:08.985
So just as I say at LingQ, we
want people to learn the language

00:06:08.985 --> 00:06:10.034
from content of interest.

00:06:10.215 --> 00:06:14.145
If you want to increase your reading, go
find things that are of interest to you.

00:06:14.205 --> 00:06:17.625
And I know that kids, there
are books that they like for

00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:21.495
whatever reason they like them,
like books by Roald Dahl that are

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:25.840
very popular with kids. And now they're
fussing about the wording in those books,

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.920
which is so silly because really all
you want is to encourage kids to read.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:35.470
Nothing that is offensive to any
particular group in any particular book is

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.380
gonna have a lasting impact on the reader.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.610
The most important thing is to
get the reader reading, and the

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:43.690
same is true in language learning.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:47.440
Read things that you enjoy and you
will improve your skills at reading.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.710
You will improve your skills if
you listen to the same content.

00:06:51.105 --> 00:06:55.125
You will improve your listening skills.
But there are steps, and if you look at

00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:59.475
my statistics at LingQ, you'll see, first
of all, that it's much easier for me to

00:06:59.475 --> 00:07:04.845
read in Polish than to read in Persian
or Arabic, even though I have spent far

00:07:04.845 --> 00:07:07.365
more time on Persian than on Polish.

00:07:07.605 --> 00:07:13.455
So it's not difficult for me to get up
to 50-, 60,000 words a day of reading in

00:07:13.455 --> 00:07:18.285
Polish when I'm really on it, whereas
it's much more difficult to do that

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:19.615
in Persian.

00:07:20.095 --> 00:07:23.635
In fact, in Polish, I can simply
read through the text, listen to

00:07:23.635 --> 00:07:27.145
it while reading if I want, or
listen to it when I'm away from the

00:07:27.145 --> 00:07:30.205
computer, come back and read it again
to pick up the words that I missed.

00:07:30.505 --> 00:07:32.755
But when I'm in Persian,
I prefer to go through

00:07:33.225 --> 00:07:38.325
the lesson in "sentence mode" where the
audio is timestamped to the sentence.

00:07:38.475 --> 00:07:43.485
I can closely connect the words to the
audio because I don't yet have that

00:07:43.785 --> 00:07:48.795
phonemic awareness in Persian because
the writing system is more obtuse to

00:07:48.795 --> 00:07:51.285
me. I'm not so used to it as I am

00:07:51.549 --> 00:07:55.119
to the Latin alphabet. And
I should add that in Polish—

00:07:55.179 --> 00:07:58.210
and I haven't spent much time in
Polish, but I was over in Poland

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:03.250
and so I imported this ebook into
LingQ and I also was given a copy.

00:08:03.255 --> 00:08:05.979
In fact, I was first given a
copy of the book in sort of book

00:08:05.979 --> 00:08:07.239
form, traditional book form.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:09.849
Then I found the
ebook and I imported it.

00:08:09.969 --> 00:08:14.419
So now I can go through reading it on
LingQ, looking up words, and then I can

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.960
sit down and enjoy the book
in a traditional book format.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:21.770
And I think this is something well
worth doing, even if you read the

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.810
same thing in different formats.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:24.860
It all contributes.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.000
It's all part of reading.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:31.070
Can I get in polish to where
I read a million words a year?

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:36.350
In other words, a book a month or
a little more than a book a month?

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:37.679
If I lived in Poland,

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:41.069
if Polish were the only language
I was learning, I think I could

00:08:41.069 --> 00:08:44.189
get there. And I think I would
make it an objective to get there.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.750
Now, if we step back to Persian,
it's so much more difficult for me.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:53.699
If I lived in Iran and if I had to get
to that level, I think I would set myself

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:59.010
the goal of trying to read a book in
Persian, you know, as a goal, one a month.

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:00.915
I think I could get there.

00:09:00.944 --> 00:09:05.204
Part of it is, when reading away
from the computer, as I did with this

00:09:05.204 --> 00:09:09.615
other book on the, the History of
Krakow, ignore the words you don't know.

00:09:09.675 --> 00:09:12.824
In other words, get yourself
to a level using, you know,

00:09:12.824 --> 00:09:14.564
reading online, looking at words.

00:09:15.135 --> 00:09:18.765
Get yourself to a level where
you can still more or less follow

00:09:18.765 --> 00:09:22.005
the gist of what you're reading
without having to look up words.

00:09:22.215 --> 00:09:27.825
Because looking up words very much slows
you down and it's much faster to do that

00:09:27.825 --> 00:09:32.475
reading online where you have, you know,
a quick reference to an online dictionary

00:09:32.535 --> 00:09:36.075
and the other functionality that's
available in an application like LingQ,

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.790
to acquire vocabulary.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:41.480
But the goal, if you are dedicated
to one language and you genuinely

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.800
want to become fluent in that
language, the goal of reading a

00:09:45.805 --> 00:09:48.710
million words a year is attainable.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.910
And I should add, because not
everything you read is gonna be a book.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.425
So if you read a book a week, You don't
have to get to the level of Elon Musk or

00:09:58.454 --> 00:10:02.535
Warren Buffet, and I might add in there
Bill Gates, who claims, I think, that he

00:10:02.535 --> 00:10:06.915
reads 50 books a year, which isn't in
the 30 million words a year, but it's

00:10:06.915 --> 00:10:08.835
in more than 3 million words a year.

00:10:08.865 --> 00:10:10.545
You don't even have to get to that level.

00:10:10.875 --> 00:10:15.975
If you read a book a week in your target
language, you will definitely surpass

00:10:16.545 --> 00:10:19.755
a million words a year because
you do reading of articles, of

00:10:19.755 --> 00:10:21.645
newspaper articles, social media.

00:10:21.705 --> 00:10:27.645
But if you are committed to becoming,
call it "C1" in your target language, then

00:10:27.645 --> 00:10:29.835
you should be able to read a book a week.

00:10:30.465 --> 00:10:33.405
And if you can combine it with
listening, so combination ebook-audiobook,

00:10:33.435 --> 00:10:35.535
if you can combine the two,

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:39.960
Because reading is so intimately—in
our brains—connected with listening,

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.370
then you are going to dramatically
improve your ability to read,

00:10:44.375 --> 00:10:45.840
your familiarity with the language.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.300
All of those things in your
brain are gonna be activated.

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:51.420
So I think that's a laudable
goal and an achievable goal.

00:10:51.704 --> 00:10:56.235
Although I admit that with me in
Persian, it's still a long way away.

00:10:56.265 --> 00:11:00.495
And because I'm not dedicated to one
language, I probably won't achieve it.

00:11:00.675 --> 00:11:05.535
But for those of you who are, you
know, determined to really kind of

00:11:05.535 --> 00:11:10.305
ratchet up your skill level in a
language, a million words a year is not

00:11:10.305 --> 00:11:12.105
a difficult goal for you to achieve.

00:11:12.105 --> 00:11:15.824
And there will be many, many
benefits across your language skills.

00:11:16.305 --> 00:11:17.175
Thank you for listening.

00:11:17.444 --> 00:11:17.954
Bye for now.

